U   U1  in1 gnd
U   U2  in2 gnd 
R   R1  in1 K_L
R   R2  in2 K_L
L   L   K_L K_Lout


/* Measure the current through L by a current probe, which doesn't have
   any impact on the circuit behavior. */
PI  I_L K_Lout out

/* The voltage drop at the current probe needs to be zero. */
DEF U_probe K_Lout out

C   C   out gnd

/* User-defined voltage are specified as voltage between two nodes. */
DEF U_LC K_L gnd

/* Let Octave plot the dependency U_out(U1) in the frequency domain. The
   default plot will show frequencies from 10 to 20000 Hz, with a density
   of 20 points/decade.
     Draw the plot by just typing U_out in Octave.
     See manual, Section 4.3.3 amd 4.3.4 for more details. */
PLOT Bode U_out U1 LOG 20 10 20k

/* Let Octave plot the step response of several characteristic voltages in
   the circuit. These can be node potentials (U_K_L, U_out) or
   user-defined voltages (U_LC).
     Please note that a node potential depends on the choice of the ground
   node. A result for a node potential is undefined if the circuit doesn't
   clearly defines the ground node but leaves the choice to linNet.
     For systems with multiple inputs, Octave presents the step responses
   by showing the selected output voltages for a step at each input in
   separate plot windows. 
     Draw the plot by just typing Res in Octave.
     See manual, Section 4.3.2 and the following for more details. */
RES Res U_out U_K_L U_LC LIN 500 10 20k

/* Plausibility check: The voltage drop at a current probe needs to be
   always zero. Let's have a dedicated user-defined result to prove it.
   Look for U_probeIsZero in the linNet log file. */
RES U_probeIsZero U_probe